{"sounds":{"intro":"Classical (Written) Mongolian is written in the traditional script: top to bottom, with the lines running left to right. It was the written language of the Mongol Empire and is still used in Inner Mongolia. The Latin shown is the scholars' transliteration, which keeps the classical spelling and the sounds that spoken Khalkha has lost.","rows":[["a / e","back and front \"a\" (a = ah, e = eh)","ger"],["o / u","back rounded vowels (o = oh, u = oo)","nom"],["ö / ü","front rounded vowels, as in German \"ö\" and \"ü\"","öglüge"],["γ","a soft, throaty \"gh\" sound, used in back-vowel words","γal"],["g","a hard \"g\", used in front-vowel words","ger"],["q","a hard \"kh\" made at the back of the mouth, in back-vowel words","qara"],["k","the same letter as q, but said as \"kh\" in front-vowel words","kümün"],["š / č / j","\"sh\", \"ch\" and \"j\"","baγši, čai, jaγun"],["ng","like \"ng\" in \"sing\"","mingγan"],["y / w","\"y\" and \"w\" glides","yeke, dawa"],["-","a hyphen shows where a suffix attaches. It is not written in the script","bayin-a"]],"notes":["Vowel harmony: the back vowels a, o, u go with q and γ. The front vowels e, ö, ü go with k and g. The vowel i is neutral. Suffixes follow the same rule.","The letters change shape at the start, in the middle and at the end of a word, and the spelling follows tradition rather than sound. The script needs a Mongolian font, which this app includes.","This pool is smaller than the Spanish one on purpose. Only forms that could be reconstructed with confidence are included, and the forms have not been checked by a native reader of the script. Treat it as a first guide."]},"numbers":[{"id":"0-to-10","title":"0 to 10","items":[["one","nigen","","ᠨᠢᠭᠡᠨ"],["two","qoyar","","ᠬᠣᠶᠠᠷ"],["three","γurban","","ᠭᠤᠷᠪᠠᠨ"],["four","dörben","","ᠳᠥᠷᠪᠡᠨ"],["five","tabun","","ᠲᠠᠪᠤᠨ"],["six","jirγuγan","","ᠵᠢᠷᠭᠤᠭᠠᠨ"],["seven","doluγan","","ᠳᠣᠯᠤᠭᠠᠨ"],["eight","naiman","","ᠨᠠᠢᠮᠠᠨ"],["nine","yisün","","ᠶᠢᠰᠦᠨ"],["ten","arban","","ᠠᠷᠪ